/perf/ab/raw/

# Machine-written, per-session local state under .claude/. That directory IS
# tracked (settings.json, settings.local.json, hooks/), so without these lines a
# plain `git add -A` sweeps one session's throwaway output into the repo
# permanently.
#
# Both files below are load-bearing for the ship gate, and ignoring them is what
# keeps it honest (arc operating-model-v2 Phase 2):
#   .review-stamp.json  gating the review receipt on itself is circular -- minting
#                       a stamp changes the stamp, which would demand a new stamp.
#   .session-lock.json  machine-local coordination state, not a guard.
#
# arc-state.json is deliberately NOT ignored. It has the same "sits in every ship's
# risky scope" problem, but it is also the file that CARRIES the declared-scope
# array that can disarm pre-ship's fatal drift check -- so hiding it would mean an
# edit widening that array never appears in a diff and never needs a receipt, and
# resolveDeclaredScope() would return "none" on CI and in every fresh clone,
# silently degrading the precise DECLARED mode to the advisory INFERRED heuristic.
# It is exempted from the risk tier instead, via TIER_EXEMPT in
# scripts/lib/review-scope.mjs: exempt without hiding.
/.claude/*-review-findings.json
/.claude/.session-lock.json
# writeLock() stages to .session-lock.json.tmp-<pid> before an atomic rename; a Windows
# EPERM on the rename can orphan one, and heartbeats rewrite the lock twice a turn.
/.claude/.session-lock.json.tmp-*
/.claude/.review-stamp.json
/.claude/.review-mint-log.json
# tests/integrityControlBytes.test.ts writes poisoned fixtures here and removes
# them in afterAll; a crashed run must not leave litter that reds other guards.
/.integrity-canary/
# tests/integrityCouldNotRun.test.ts writes its own fixtures, and needs a
# directory of its own: vitest runs spec files in parallel workers and the
# sibling above removes its directory recursively in afterAll.
/.integrity-canary-cnr/
# scripts/mutate-workflow-artifact-policy.mjs writes a deliberately-broken copy
# of a guard next to the original and unlinks it in a finally; Ctrl-C or a
# timeout in between leaves one behind.
#
# THIS RULE ONLY KEEPS IT OUT OF `git add -A`. An earlier version of this comment
# claimed it stopped the scanners too, and that is false: check-script-conventions
# walks the filesystem with readdir and consults no gitignore, so leftover litter
# still reds `npm run lint` -- and now under a name `git status` will not show
# you, which is worse than not ignoring it. What actually clears it is the
# startup sweep in the harness itself. (check-integrity.sh was measured and does
# NOT flag one, so that half of the old comment was wrong in the other
# direction.)
/scripts/.mutant-*.mjs
